= Wave 14 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

Wave 14 completes the concept-wave program with a coherent scholarly-communication and open-research layer connecting epistemology, scientific method, provenance, metadata, reproducibility, and computational research.

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Scientific literature ===
The scientific literature is the body of recorded scientific works through which research questions, methods, evidence, analyses, results, interpretations, and critiques are communicated.

=== Scholarly communication ===
Scholarly communication is the system of processes, institutions, technologies, and practices through which research is created, evaluated, disseminated, discovered, preserved, discussed, and reused.

=== Peer review ===
Peer review is the evaluation of research work, proposals, or scholarly outputs by people with relevant expertise before or after dissemination.

=== Citation ===
A citation is an explicit reference from one scholarly work or research object to another source, contribution, dataset, software artifact, or prior work.

=== Academic publishing ===
Academic publishing is the set of processes and institutions used to prepare, evaluate, produce, register, distribute, and preserve scholarly works.

=== Preprint ===
A preprint is a complete public draft of a scholarly or scientific work made available before it has been certified by a journal or conference peer-review process.

=== Open access ===
Open access is an access and reuse model for scholarly outputs that makes them available online without price barriers and, under stronger definitions, with broad permissions for lawful reuse.

=== Open science ===
Open science is a broad set of practices and infrastructures intended to make scientific knowledge, tools, methods, data, and processes more accessible, transparent, reusable, collaborative, and participatory where appropriate.

=== Dataset ===
A dataset is an organized collection of related data treated as a unit for storage, analysis, exchange, citation, or reuse.

=== Research data management ===
Research data management is the planning and practice of organizing, documenting, storing, protecting, preserving, accessing, and sharing research data across its lifecycle.

=== Data repository ===
A data repository is an infrastructure or service for depositing, preserving, describing, discovering, accessing, and stewarding datasets or other research data.

=== FAIR data ===
FAIR data are data managed according to the FAIR Guiding Principles so that they are more Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable for people and machines.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
scientific literature ≠ all scientific knowledge
scholarly communication ≠ academic publishing only
peer review ≠ correctness
citation ≠ endorsement
citation count ≠ quality
academic publishing ≠ peer review
preprint ≠ peer-reviewed article
open access ≠ open science
free-to-read ≠ unrestricted reuse
open science ≠ compulsory disclosure of all research
dataset ≠ database
research data management ≠ sharing only
data repository ≠ database
repository deposit ≠ preservation guarantee
FAIR ≠ open
FAIR ≠ data quality
FAIR ≠ one technical standard
</pre>

== Current-state notes ==

* UNESCO's Recommendation on Open Science was adopted 23 November 2021.
* The NIH Data Management and Sharing Policy remains the 2023 policy; 2026 changes simplify the required DMS Plan format rather than replace the policy.
* FAIR explicitly permits controlled access where authentication or authorization is necessary.

== Reader-linking rule ==

Core pages link only to canonical Core identities that already exist or are created in this wave. Simple/Technical surfaces remain derivatives and do not duplicate Knowledge object/category identity.

== Redlink policy ==

Future descendants captured in Phase 1 remain coverage signals, not automatic stub authorization.

